    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d596">

                <pb facs="0654" n="644" sameAs="frus:frus1944v04/pg644"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00112 European War 1939/10–644:
                    Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Sweden</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Stockholm</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-10-06T21:00:00+01:00">October 6, 1944—9
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 10:22 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>4066. For the Department only. Legation’s 4053, October 6, 2 p.m.,<anchor n="86"
                        xml:id="d596-f1"/> 1221 to London for the Ambassador. In presence of Larson
                    and Winquist, two directors of SKF, Hamberg assured Waring and Ravndal,
                    following extended but affable conversation this afternoon, <pb facs="0655"
                        n="645" xml:id="pg645"/> that he would recommend to Board of Directors that
                    SKF completely stop all shipments of ball and roller bearings and component
                    parts and machinery to enemy Europe effective October 12. He was urged to obtain
                    Board’s formal approval immediately and to notify us thereof by Monday<anchor
                        n="87" xml:id="d596-f2"/> at the latest. This he undertook to do.</p>
                <p>Hamberg stated that the SKF would need evidence of threatened punitive action by
                    Allied Governments for use in satisfying Germans, that complete stoppage of
                    exports was result of “economical <hi rendition="#italic">force majeure</hi>”.
                    Documents in language which company believes will best serve purpose is being
                    drafted by company’s lawyer. It is thought that company’s decision will not be
                    qualified by conditions. It will, however, be understood that we will reach an
                    interpretation of the indemnity clause of the June Agreement and also give
                    assurances regarding SKF properties in Germany and France.</p>
                <p>My 1225, October 6, 9 p.m. repeats this to London, for the Ambassador only.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
